          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Capture all opponent stones or block the opponent so they have no legal moves.',
          gameplay: 'Two players take turns moving their stones on a square grid. Pieces move in all directions (orthogonally, one square or sliding like a rook). Capture is by custodian capture: when an opponent stone is flanked on two opposite sides by your stones, it is captured and removed. Win by capturing all opponent stones or blocking the opponent so they have no legal moves. No random elements; pure strategy.',
          keyRules: [
            'Pieces move orthogonally on the board, one square or sliding along a row or column.',
            'Capture by surrounding an opponent piece on two opposite sides (custodian capture).',
            'Win by capturing all opponent pieces or blocking all legal moves.',
            'Keep stones in formation; isolated pieces are vulnerable to pincer capture.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Keep your stones together! A line of 3 stones is impossible to capture from the front. If you leave a lone stone in the middle of the board, your opponent will easily slide two pieces around it to 'Pincer' it into oblivion.",
          intermediate: "The 'Sacrificial Bait': Move a stone into a position where it can be captured. When the opponent moves their piece to complete the capture, use your next turn to capture BOTH the opponent's pieces that were closing in. This 'Counter-Pincer' is the core of Petteia combat.",
          advanced: "Positional Strangle: Don't focus only on capturing pieces. Focus on 'Territory Control'. If you can block your opponent's access to the center of the board, their pieces will be stuck on the edges where they have fewer liberties. Use your stones to create 'Walls' that force the opponent into a corner where they can be captured more effectively.",
          tips: [
            "The 'Greek Slide': Because pieces can move any distance, look for long-range captures from across the board.",
            'If you are playing the 12x8 board, prioritize the flanks; captures on the edges are harder to avoid but easier to execute.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
